notes:
VERIFIED IN A BROWSER (2026-08-23), the bug is real:
- The data-select handler in docs/design/mocks/shared/components/walkthrough.js:201-210 clears sel/selc from EVERY .lrow,.libtile,.filmcard,.clip,.editpt,.xband in the stage, then lights only the step targets.
- pages/composites.html: authored .filmcard.sel at line 241 inside .filmstrip#histStrip[data-radio]. At load the card is selected; after ONE click of .wnext (step 2, data-select=#lrFrame) no .filmcard carries sel and all four .acts rows compute display:none. The history pane loses its action row for the rest of the walkthrough.
- pages/component-configure-wt.html: authored .filmcard.sel at line 182; by step 1 (data-select=#lrGroup) nothing in the strip is selected and all eight .acts rows are display:none, so this page never shows the action row at all once wt initialises.
- pages/capture-wt.html is NOT affected: its history steps carry data-select=#fcNew, and the later layer-row steps run after data-sheet-shut=#histSheet. Worth knowing, because capture-wt is the clickthrough closest to the measure-redline focus.
- 19 pages combine a .wt with filmcards and data-select (agent-generate-wt, capture-wt, component-configure-wt, composites, ds-build-wt, icon-draw-wt, icon-gradient-wt, img-grade-wt, img-retouch-wt, ui-build-screen, vector-wt, the seven video-*-wt, walk.html). Only the ones that author a .filmcard.sel in an always-visible strip show the damage.

CORRECTED RECIPE (validated in-page on composites.html). Do NOT use the original idea of excluding [data-radio] strips from the sweep: that breaks acceptance item 2, because capture-wt legitimately selects INSIDE such a strip and would lose exclusivity there. Scope the sweep by group instead:
  var SEL = '.lrow,.libtile,.filmcard,.clip,.editpt,.xband';
  var groupOf = function (el) { return el.closest('[data-radio]') || stage; };
  var targets = list(v);
  var groups = new Set(targets.map(groupOf));
  groups.forEach(function (g) {
    all(SEL, g).forEach(function (r) { if (groupOf(r) === g) r.classList.remove('sel', 'selc'); });
  });
  targets.forEach(function (pick) { pick.classList.add(/* unchanged class resolution */); });
Measured result on composites: the filmstrip keeps its .sel card AND .lrow.sel is exactly [lrGroup], so panel exclusivity survives.
Why the stage fallback matters: layer rows sit in no [data-radio], so their group is the whole stage, which preserves the documented multi-surface behaviour (one step marks the same selection in every surface that shows it, found-selection). A step listing targets in several groups clears the union of those groups, so cross-surface steps still work.
Follow-up when this is implemented: re-check the 19 pages above, and add a case to shared/check-selection.mjs so a stage-wide sweep cannot silently come back.
